28
00:03:04,804 --> 00:03:09,955
There's no three attempts as there is in the high jump or the long jump.

29
00:03:09,975 --> 00:03:11,919
It's one strike and you're out, really.

30
00:03:14,228 --> 00:03:20,078
judges being introduced they've got the toughest job of a lot in here as this standard builds in the competition.

31
00:03:20,459 --> 00:03:32,439
Seven of them have to judge the execution of each dive which is of course is pre-announced and given a specific degree of difficulty or tariff.

32
00:03:32,419 --> 00:03:40,299
You'll be able to follow this quite simply when the captions after each diver is put on your screen for you.

33
00:03:40,318 --> 00:03:48,116
The top two and the bottom two marks are discounted and those three remaining marks are then multiplied by that degree of difficulty.

34
00:03:48,097 --> 00:04:10,122
and the basic rule is that the higher the degree of difficulty the bigger the prize certainly with the lower degrees of difficulties you've got to execute them near perfectly to make sure you maintain the sort of point score you'll need to and it's very interesting to see Steve as it goes through the order they go in a one or two of them coming out playing their big dives early on hoping to

35
00:04:10,102 --> 00:04:13,650
post a good score and one or two others starting a bit more conservatively.

36
00:04:14,472 --> 00:04:17,519
And I just think that's a matter of courses for courses.

37
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:24,154
